// A `fn ref T` may root its place in STATIC STORAGE — the third sound root, alongside `this` and a `ref`
// parameter (KR-66 widened the rule; SPEC *Writing a collection in kama*).
//
// It was refused until then, and refused with a message that told the author their correct program would
// dangle: `static int32 g; fn ref int32 counter() { return g; }` -> "a `ref T` result must borrow `this` or
// a `ref` parameter". The rule listed the roots it had been taught, and a static was simply not among them
// — yet it is the SAFEST of the three: `this` and a `ref` param outlive the call only because the CALLER
// holds them, while a static outlives every frame unconditionally. Rust spells it `&'static`, C++ returns
// one from a Meyers singleton, Go takes the address of a package-level var; none of them needs a carve-out.
//
// Exercised as read, write, compound assignment and a `ref` argument, like tests/reffn_at.kama does for the
// `ref`-parameter root. The sibling that must STILL be refused is tests/xfail/reffn_local.kama (a local
// root would dangle) and tests/xfail/reffn_static_local.kama (a local that merely shares a static's name).
type value Counters {
    public int32 hits;
    public int32 misses;
}

static int32 g_ticks;
static Counters g_counters;

fn ref int32 ticks() { return g_ticks; }
fn ref int32 hits() { return g_counters.hits; }        // a FIELD of a static root, as `this.f` is of `this`
fn void bump(ref int32 v) { v = v + 100; }

fn int32 main() {
    ticks() = 5;
    ticks() += 2;                  // 7
    hits() = 1;
    bump(v: ref hits());           // 101 — the place passes as a `ref` argument
    g_counters.misses = 3;         // written directly, read back through the place below
    return ticks() + hits() + g_counters.misses;   // 7 + 101 + 3 = 111
}
